00:30:10.000 --> 00:30:51.000
All air and seaports to Ireland were sealed as James Hanratty's boyish face appeared in newspapers. Despite an extensive search, including speaking with a man who wrote postcards on behalf of James to send to his mother, detectives returned to England on October 5 empty-handed. At the time, James Hanratty was living it up in Soho, having returned from Ireland in mid-September. A proficient driver, James had received his driver's licence with no problems. He befriended an antique dealer and stayed in her apartment.
随着詹姆斯·汉拉蒂稚气未脱的脸庞出现在各大报纸上，所有通往爱尔兰的航空和海港均被封锁。尽管进行了广泛的搜查——包括与一名代詹姆斯写明信片寄给他母亲的男子交谈，侦探们仍于10月5日空手返回英格兰。当时，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂在苏荷区过着挥金如土的生活，他已于9月中旬从爱尔兰返回。作为一名熟练的司机，詹姆斯顺利拿到了驾照。他与一位古董商交上了朋友，并住在她的公寓里。

45
00:30:51.000 --> 00:31:41.000
James spent considerable money from his proceeds of crime to buy a car. He would wine and dine women and take them to the theatre at the London Palladium. On October 5, 1961, James Hanratty's luck changed. He was at one of his favourite local hangouts, the rehearsal club, when an acquaintance told him that officers were snooping around, looking for him in relation to the A6 shooting. Pale faced and panicked, James rang his good friend, Charles Frantz. Charles tried to calm James, telling him he had nothing to worry about and to go to Scotland Yard to clear his name.
詹姆斯用犯罪所得的大笔资金买了一辆车。他带着女性饮酒作乐，带她们去伦敦帕拉丁剧院（London Palladium）看戏。1961年10月5日，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂的好运到头了。他在自己最喜欢的当地聚集地之一排练俱乐部（Rehearsal Club）时，一个熟人告诉他，警察正在四处搜寻他，调查与A6枪击案有关的事情。面色苍白、惊慌失措的詹姆斯给他的好朋友查尔斯·弗兰茨打了个电话。查尔斯试图安抚詹姆斯，告诉他不用担心，并让他去苏格兰场以清白。

46
00:31:41.000 --> 00:32:20.000
Hanratty abruptly hung up. Charles Frantz was dismayed. He hadn't kept James on the line long enough for his call to be traced. The following day, James Hanratty phoned Scotland Yard from a public phone booth. He spoke to Detective Acott, explaining that he was innocent. However, he couldn't come forward, as he was wanted for housebreaking, after his fingerprints had been found at a crime scene the month prior. James Hanratty told the detective he couldn't take another prison stint, but desperately
汉拉蒂粗暴地挂断了电话。查尔斯·弗兰茨感到沮丧。他没能让詹姆斯在线足够长的时间来追踪他的通话。第二天，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂从一个公用电话亭给苏格兰场打电话。他与阿科特侦探通话，解释说自己是无辜的。然而，他不能自首，因为他因上个月在一个犯罪现场发现了他的指纹而被通缉犯下入室盗窃罪。詹姆斯·汉拉蒂告诉侦探，他经受不起再次入狱的折磨，但极其渴望……

47
00:32:20.000 --> 00:33:02.000
wanted the matter of his alleged involvement in the A6 attack cleared up. James then called the newspaper officers of the Daily Mirror and spoke with an assistant editor. Hanratty explained his predicament and professed his innocence, telling the editor he had a watertight alibi for the night in question. Later that night, James Hanratty made another call to Detective Acott. Acott pleaded for Hanratty to turn himself in for questioning, but again he refused. The next afternoon, Hanratty called Detective Acott again.
澄清他涉嫌参与A6袭击案的事情。随后，詹姆斯打电话给《每日镜报》（Daily Mirror）报社办公室，并与一位助理编辑进行了交谈。汉拉蒂解释了自己的困境并坚称自己无辜，告诉编辑他有案发当晚的坚固不在场证明。那天晚些时候，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂再次致电阿科特侦探。阿科特恳求汉拉蒂自首配合调查，但他再次拒绝了。第二天下午，汉拉蒂再次致电阿科特侦探。

48
00:33:02.000 --> 00:33:44.000
He elaborated on his alibi further, telling the detective that he had spent the night of August 21 at the Vienna Hotel under the alias Jim Ryan. The next day, when the A6 attack took place, he had taken a train 200 miles north to Liverpool to stay with friends. He had been there for three nights before returning to London. Hanratty explained three men could vouch for him, but they would not do so as they themselves were criminals. Detectives drove to Liverpool and searched for anyone who might admit to being Hanratty's
他进一步阐述了自己的不在场证明，告诉侦探他在8月21日晚以吉姆·瑞安的化名住在维也纳酒店。第二天，也就是A6袭击发生时，他坐火车向北行了两百英里前往利物浦与朋友会合。他在那里待了三个晚上才返回伦敦。汉拉蒂解释说有三个人可以为他作证，但他们不会这么做，因为他们自己就是罪犯。侦探们开车前往利物浦，寻找任何可能承认是汉拉蒂不在场证明证人的人。

49
00:33:44.000 --> 00:34:25.000
alibi. They had no luck. On October 11, four days after his last contact with Detective Acott, James Hanratty entered a cafe in Blackpool, a seaside town 50 miles north of Liverpool. Confident he'd be safe from the officers searching for him in London, Hanratty ordered a meal and took a seat near the jukebox. He bowed his head when two police officers entered the cafe. Upon exiting the cafe, James Hanratty was promptly arrested.
他们没有找到任何人。10月11日，即他最后一次与阿科特侦探联系四天后，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂走进了一家位于利物浦以北50英里的海滨小镇黑潭（Blackpool）的一家咖啡馆。坚信自己能避开在伦敦搜寻他的警察，汉拉蒂点了一餐并在点唱机旁坐下。当两名警察走进咖啡馆时，他低下了头。刚一走出咖啡馆，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂就被迅速逮捕。

50
00:34:25.000 --> 00:35:02.000
He told the policeman his name was Peter Bates, but after arriving at Blackpool police station, he confessed to being James Hanratty. Detective Acott rushed to the station, arriving at seven the next morning to interview his prime suspect. Although the Hanratty family had a trusted family lawyer, James was not given the option of having him present. According to author Paul Foote, Detective Acott lent over a desk and spoke to Hanratty about the Vienna Hotel, saying,
他告诉警察自己的名字叫彼得·贝茨（Peter Bates），但在抵达黑潭警察局后，他承认自己是詹姆斯·汉拉蒂。阿科特侦探匆忙赶往警察局，于第二天早上七点到达，开始讯问他的主要嫌疑人。尽管汉拉蒂家族有一位受信任的家族律师，但詹姆斯并没有被给予让律师在场的选择。据作家保罗·福特（Paul Foote）称，阿科特侦探俯身越过桌子，对汉拉蒂谈到了维也纳酒店，说道：

51
00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:43.000
I can't make it too clear how desperate your position is. I must tell you now, after you're leaving room 24 on August 22, two empty cartridge cases were found at the end of the bed you tell me you slept in that night. James replied, You're kidding me Mr. Acott, aren't you? In response, Detective Acott said, I'm not kidding you Jimmy, this is very serious business. The only other person present in the interview room was another detective who took notes
“我无法向你阐明你的处境有多么绝望。我必须现在告诉你，在你于8月22日离开24号房间后，在你跟我说你当晚睡觉的那张床的床头发现了两个空弹壳。”詹姆斯回答道：“你在跟我开玩笑吧，阿科特先生，对吧？”对此，阿科特侦探说：“吉米，我不是在跟你开玩笑，这是一件非常严重的事情。”讯问室里仅有的另一个人是另一名侦探，他负责做记录……

52
00:35:43.000 --> 00:36:28.000
of what went on. When the interview concluded, these notes were not signed by or read to James Hanratty to ensure they were an accurate representation of what was discussed from his perspective. James then told the officers in his heavy cockney accent that he was off to have a good kip, explaining he was tired and could sleep anywhere. The next day, those who witnessed the Morris Minor driving in the hours before and after the shooting were asked to view an identification parade to pinpoint the offender. Standing before them, amongst several decoys, was James Hanratty.
记录事情的经过。讯问结束后，这些记录未经詹姆斯·汉拉蒂签名或宣读，以确保它们准确反映了从他的角度所讨论的内容。随后，詹姆斯用他浓重的伦敦东区口音告诉警察，他要去找个舒服的地方好好睡一觉，并解释说自己很累，在哪里都能睡着。第二天，那些在枪击发生前几小时和后几小时目击莫里斯矿工汽车行驶的人被要求参加辨认队列以找出罪犯。站在他们面前、夹在几个诱饵中间的人正是詹姆斯·汉拉蒂。

53
00:36:28.000 --> 00:37:10.000
Two out of three witnesses picked James as being in the car. A third witness, the pump attendant who fuelled the car shortly before the shooting, pointed out someone else. Still, there was one other person who could shed light on James Hanratty's involvement in the A6 attack. Barely surviving the ordeal that killed her partner, Valerie Story had spent the past seven weeks in hospital recovering. After being raped and gunned down, Valerie Story lay in shock beside the body of her
三名证人中有两人指认詹姆斯在车里。第三位证人——在枪击发生前不久为汽车加油的加油工，指认了另一个人。尽管如此，还有另一个人可以阐明詹姆斯·汉拉蒂对A6袭击案的参与情况。瓦莱丽·斯托里（Valerie Storie）在夺走她伴侣生命的劫难中险死还生，过去七周一直在医院康复。在遭到强暴和枪击后，瓦莱丽·斯托里震惊地躺在男友迈克尔·格雷格斯顿的尸体旁。

54
00:37:10.000 --> 00:37:47.000
boyfriend Michael Gregston. She couldn't move her legs. The killer had driven away in Michael's Morris Minor. Valerie desperately tried to get the attention of passing motorists by waving her petticoat, but she was concealed from the road by a grassy knoll. Fearing she was going to die, Valerie made the words blue eyes and brown hair out of pebbles to give authorities clues to her and Michael's assailant. After hearing Valerie's pained groans, a farm laborer discovered the couple and sought
她的双腿动弹不得。凶手驾驶着迈克尔的莫里斯矿工汽车逃跑了。瓦莱丽绝望地挥舞着衬裙，试图引起过往司机的注意，但她被草地丘陵挡住，从路上看不到她。由于害怕自己会死，瓦莱丽用卵石拼出了“蓝色眼睛”和“棕色眼睛”这几个字，为当局提供关于她和迈克尔袭击者的线索。听到瓦莱丽痛苦的呻吟后，一名农场工人发现了这对情侣并寻求……

55
00:37:47.000 --> 00:38:22.000
help. Council worker John Kerr arrived and flagged down the first car that drove past, telling them to call an ambulance. It was clear to John that Michael had been deceased for some time, so he focused his attention on Valerie. John wrote down Valerie's recollection of the attack on the back of his work papers, in case she passed away before speaking with authorities. According to John, Valerie described the man responsible as a little taller than she was,
帮助。市政工人约翰·克尔（John Kerr）赶到并拦下了第一辆开过的汽车，告诉他们叫救护车。约翰很清楚迈克尔已经去世一段时间了，于是他把注意力集中在瓦莱丽身上。约翰将瓦莱丽对袭击的回忆写在他的工作文件背面，以防她在与当局交谈前去世。据约翰说，瓦莱丽将肇事男子描述为比她稍高一些，

56
00:38:22.000 --> 00:38:59.000
with large, staring eyes and light, fairish hair. John turned his notes over to the first police officer who arrived, but they were eventually misplaced. Valerie's story was taken to hospital in a serious condition. Two bullets were lodged in her body. One had struck her spinal cord, paralyzing her from the waist down. This also meant Valerie couldn't feel pain from her injuries, so she was able to speak coherently to detectives.
有着一双大而圆睁的眼睛，以及浅色、偏金的头发。约翰把他的笔记交给了第一个到达的警官，但这些笔记最终被弄丢了。瓦莱丽伤势严重，被送往医院。她体内嵌入了两颗子弹。其中一颗击中了她的脊髓，导致她腰部以下瘫痪。这也意味着瓦莱丽感觉不到伤痛，因此她能够逻辑清晰地与侦探交谈。

57
00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:48.000
They noted that her attacker was a man in his 30s, 5'6", with a medium build. However, inconsistencies were gradually emerging. Valerie herself had written in pebbles that her attacker had blue eyes and brown hair. One of her rescuers noted her saying that he had light, fairish hair, and the police, following their initial interviews at Valerie's bedside, reported to the press that the offender had dark brown hair and deeply set dark brown eyes. These inconsistencies would become a lingering issue throughout the investigation. No one could accurately explain why they existed.
他们记录下袭击者是一名30多岁、身高5英尺6英寸、中等身材的男子。然而，前后矛盾之处逐渐显现。瓦莱丽本人曾用鹅卵石写道，她的袭击者拥有蓝色的眼睛和棕色的头发。其中一位施救者注意到她说袭击者有着浅色、偏金的头发，而警方在瓦莱丽床边进行初步讯问后向媒体通报称，罪犯拥有深棕色的头发和深陷的深棕色眼睛。这些矛盾之处将成为贯穿整个调查的挥之不去的问题。没有人能准确解释它们为什么会存在。

58
00:39:48.000 --> 00:40:28.000
Three days later, Valerie assisted in the creation of an identikit of her attacker. A second sketch was also made based on the collective recollections of all those who had seen the driver of the Morris Minor in the hours after the attack. As this sketch was an amalgamation of multiple witness accounts, it looked very different from the one Valerie provided. This was a notable flaw in the identikit process. The human expression can be very difficult to capture. If multiple people contribute their descriptions, the result is often an image vastly different
三天后，瓦莱丽协助警方绘制了袭击者的模拟画像。根据袭击发生后几小时内所有见过那辆莫里斯·Minor轿车司机的人的共同回忆，警方还制作了第二张素描。由于这张素描是多份目击证词的结合体，它看起来与瓦莱丽提供的画像大不相同。这是模拟画像制作过程中的一个显著缺陷。人类的表情很难捕捉。如果有多人提供他们的描述，结果往往会产生一张与嫌疑人实际面孔大相径庭的图像

59
00:40:28.000 --> 00:41:08.000
to the actual face of the subject. Despite this issue, there was one similarity between both sketches. The offender's right eyebrow was raised higher than his left. Both images were circulated in the media in the hope someone would recognise the wanted man. The Liverpool Echo commented that each sketch was dominated by dark, staring eyes. This descriptor was withdrawn on August 31 for reasons unknown. Now, the offender had blue eyes.
与真实长相大相径庭。尽管存在这个问题，这两张素描之间还是有一个相似之处。罪犯的右眉毛比左眉毛抬得更高。这两幅画像都在媒体上广为流传，希望有人能认出这名通缉犯。《利物浦回声报》评论说，这两张素描的共同特点都是一双黑色的、直勾勾的眼睛。出于未知原因，这一面部特征描述在8月31日被撤回。现在，罪犯被描述为拥有蓝色的眼睛。

60
00:41:08.000 --> 00:41:49.000
Regardless of the inconsistencies, when Charlotte France saw the sketches on television, she was overcome with a sense of recognition. The man was sitting beside her, watching the same news report. When she looked at the sketches, she saw James Hanratty. Following the arrest of James Hanratty, he was brought to Valerie's hospital bedside to participate in an identity parade. The matter was urgent, as Valerie had confided to Detective Acott. My memory of this man's face is fading.
尽管存在这些矛盾之处，当夏洛蒂·弗朗斯在电视上看到这些素描时，她涌起一股强烈的熟悉感。那个男人正坐在她身边，看着同一条新闻报道。当她看着这些素描时，她看到了詹姆斯·汉拉蒂。詹姆斯·汉拉蒂被捕后，被带到瓦莱丽的医院病床前参加列队辨认。事情很紧急，正如瓦莱丽向阿科特侦探吐露的那样：“我对这个男人脸部的记忆正在消退。”

61
00:41:49.000 --> 00:42:28.000
I'm so afraid that when confronted with the man, I may not be able to pick him out. Valerie stared intently at the twelve men standing at her bedside. For ten minutes, she scrutinised their every feature and movement. She whispered to Detective Acott, who in turn instructed the men to say the line, Be quiet will you, I am thinking. One by one, they repeated the sentence. Valerie looked at them one more time and said the number of the man she thought was her attacker.
“我很害怕，当面对这个男人时，我可能认不出他来。”瓦莱丽紧紧盯着站在她病床前的十二个男人。十分钟里，她仔细审视了他们的每一个特征和动作。她对阿科特侦探耳语，阿科特随之指示这些人说出这句台词：“安静好吗，我在思考。”他们一个接一个地重复了这句话。瓦莱丽再次看了他们一眼，说出了她认为是袭击者的那个男人的编号。

62
00:42:28.000 --> 00:43:06.000
This man had said thinking instead of thinking in his thick cockney accent, just like her attacker had. As the men filed out, Detective Acott squeezed her arm and said, Well done. Valerie had picked James Hanratty. When James began housebreaking, he dyed his red hair black under the impression it would hide his identity. At the identity parade, James had very noticeable regrowth.
这个男人用他浓重的伦敦东区口音把“thinking”（思考）说成了“tinkin”，就像她的袭击者一样。当这些男人鱼贯而出时，阿科特侦探捏了捏她的手臂说：“干得好。”瓦莱丽认出了詹姆斯·汉拉蒂。当詹姆斯开始入室盗窃时，他把红发染成了黑色，以为这样就能隐藏身份。在列队辨认时，詹姆斯的头发有非常明显的新长出的发根。

63
00:43:06.000 --> 00:43:47.000
After the hospital lights, his hair looked a very unusual colour. It was also heavily reported in newspapers that police were looking for a suspect who had natural red hair, but may have dyed it black. It was unclear whether Valerie had read this description prior to the parade. James Hanratty approached Detective Acott and asked him if it was fair to be put on parade with his hair in the condition it was. Acott's reply was, I did not ask you to get your hair dyed, Jimmy. If you have any complaints, you can tell the judge.
在医院的灯光下，他的头发看起来颜色非常诡异。报纸上也大量报道说，警方正在寻找一名天生红发但可能染成黑色的嫌疑人。目前尚不清楚瓦莱丽在列队辨认前是否看过这个描述。詹姆斯·汉拉蒂走近阿科特侦探，质问他顶着一头这种状态的头发来参加列队辨认是否公平。阿科特回答说：“我可没让你去染发，吉米。如果你有什么不满，你可以去跟法官说。”

64
00:43:47.000 --> 00:44:34.000
Two days later, James Hanratty appeared in court handcuffed to a detective. He vehemently denied his involvement in the A6 shooting. In response to the charges, all he had to say was, Alright. Just over one month later, a magistrate sent James for trial at the Old Bailey in London. At the time, the legal system only tried someone for the most serious of the crimes they had committed if they were charged with multiple crimes. Because of this, all charges for the attack on Valerie's story were dropped, and James Hanratty only faced trial for the murder of Michael Gregston.
两天后，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂戴着手铐被一名侦探押着出庭。他对A6公路枪击案的涉案指控予以坚决否认。对于这些指控，他唯一的表态是：“好吧。”一个多月后，一名地方法官将詹姆斯移送至伦敦的老贝利法院受审。当时，如果嫌疑人面临多项罪名起诉，法律系统只会就其犯下的最严重罪行进行审判。正因如此，针对瓦莱丽遇袭案的所有指控都被撤销了，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂仅因谋杀迈克尔·格雷格斯顿一案而面临审判。

65
00:44:34.000 --> 00:45:11.000
As detailed by author Paul Foot, a prison medical officer assessed the James Hanratty prior to trial. He noted, He is, in my opinion, quite fit to plead. He knows the difference between right and wrong, and must be held responsible for his actions. Crime correspondence covering the case indicated that James Hanratty would likely be acquitted due to the lack of evidence against him. The main evidence to be used by the prosecution was Valerie's story's identification of him,
正如作家保罗·富特所详细描述的那样，一名监狱医疗官在审判前对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂进行了评估。他指出：“依我之见，他完全适合出庭受审。他分得清是非曲直，必须为自己的行为负责。”报道该案的犯罪记者指出，由于缺乏对不利于他的证据，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂很可能会被无罪释放。控方将使用的主要证据是瓦莱丽对他的辨认，

66
00:45:11.000 --> 00:45:55.000
the fact he used the word KIP for NAP, and his Cockney accent. Certain himself that he would be found not guilty, James signed a deal with the Daily Express for £5,000 for the exclusive to his story once the trial was over. He wrote letters through a scribe to his parents, relatives and friends professing his innocence. He assured his family that they had nothing to worry about. An excerpt of a letter taken from the book by Paul Foot reads, Well mum, I know it must be hard for you and dad right now, but I want you to have faith in me.
他用“KIP”代替“NAP”的用词习惯，以及他的伦敦东区口音。詹姆斯确信自己会被判无罪，于是与《每日快报》签下一笔5000英镑的协议，买断了他在审判结束后独家讲述自己故事的版权。他通过代笔人给父母、亲戚和朋友写信，坚称自己是清白的。他向家人保证他们没什么好担心的。保罗·富特的书中所摘录的一封信写道：“好吧，妈妈，我知道你和爸爸现在一定很难过，但我希望你们对我有信心。”

67
00:45:55.000 --> 00:46:30.000
If I had done this terrible thing, I would have took my own life weeks ago. As you know, I love you and dad very much. After I left home, I was thinking of you all the time and I am not saying that just because I am in trouble. But I have got faith in my defence. They are both very intelligent men and without any doubt they know I am innocent. As I said before, please have faith in me. I will soon straighten this terrible thing out.
“如果我做了这件可怕的事，我几个星期前就会自杀。如你们所知，我非常爱您和爸爸。我离开家后，无时无刻不在想念你们，我这么说绝不仅仅是因为我陷入了麻烦。但我对我的辩护团队有信心。他们都是非常聪明的人，毫无疑问，他们知道我是清白的。正如我之前所说，请对我有信心。我很快就会把这桩可怕的事澄清。”

68
00:46:30.000 --> 00:47:18.000
In order to have the case heard sooner, on January 2, 1962, it was decided that the trial would be moved from London to where the A6 murder occurred. This was a huge setback for James Henraddy. It meant the jury would be comprised of Bedford locals, many of whom had thorough knowledge of and were still outraged by the attack. The trial began on January 22, 1962, near five months after the crime. On the third day, James Henraddy shocked his defence team. He had a confession to make.
为了让案件能更早开庭审理，1962年1月2日，法院决定将审判地点从伦敦转移到A6谋杀案发生的地点。这对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂来说是一个巨大的挫折。这意味着陪审团将由贝德福德当地人组成，他们许多人对这起袭击事件有深入的了解，并且至今仍感到愤怒。审判于1962年1月22日开始，距离案发将近五个月。在审判的第三天，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂震惊了他的辩护团队。他有一个供词要交代。

69
00:47:18.000 --> 00:48:03.000
James admitted he had lied to detectives about his alibi. He had not been in Liverpool with three criminals, but instead by himself in the Welsh seaside town of Rill, 43 miles west of Liverpool. He told Detective Acott he was in Liverpool as he thought his three criminal friends would vouch for him and he took a train there to offer them £250 to give him an alibi. When his friends refused, not wanting to become involved, James Henraddy was in a bind. He didn't want to say he was in Rill because there was nobody there to verify it, so he stuck with his Liverpool alibi.
詹姆斯承认他曾向侦探们撒谎，隐瞒了自己的不在场证明。他当时并没有和三个罪犯在利物浦，而是独自一人在利物浦以西43英里处的威尔士海滨小镇雷尔。他告诉阿科特侦探说自己在利物浦，是因为他以为那三个犯罪朋友会为他作证，于是他坐火车去那里出价250英镑让他们为他提供不在场证明。当他的朋友们因为不想卷入其中而拒绝时，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂陷入了困境。他不想说自己在雷尔，因为那里没有人能证实，所以他坚持了利物浦的不在场证明。

70
00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:47.000
Now however, he had a change of heart. Although his legal team warned him that changing his alibi made him look guilty, James said he did not want to take the stand unless he could tell the truth. His defence barrister conceded. As far as he was concerned, James was well regarded as a good-natured, likable person. He felt it was important for the jury to meet James, regardless of his changing alibi. Valerie's story was wheeled into court in a hospital bed. The damage to her spinal cord meant that she would spend the rest of her life in a wheelchair.
然而现在，他改变了主意。尽管他的法律团队警告他，改变不在场证明会让他看起来像个罪犯，但詹姆斯表示，除非能说出真相，否则他拒绝出庭作证。他的辩护大律师妥协了。在他看来，詹姆斯一向被认为是一个性格随和、讨人喜欢的人。他觉得无论不在场证明如何改变，让陪审团见见詹姆斯是很重要的。瓦莱丽·斯托里躺在医院的病床上被推入法庭。她脊髓受到的损伤意味着她将在轮椅上度过余生。

71
00:48:47.000 --> 00:49:38.000
Her testimony was read aloud, part of which included the statement. While I was facing him after he shot Mike, a car came up from behind and lit up his face. He seemed to be staring through me with very large, icy blue eyes. The defence team picked apart Valerie's recollections and the different descriptions she allegedly gave of her attacker's physical appearance, specifically the colour of his hair and eyes. All the written records of her describing her assailant had since been lost, so records of what she said could not be backed up in court. The cartridge cases found in the Vienna hotel room where James Hanratty stayed were debated
她的证词被当庭宣读，其中部分内容包含这样的陈述：“在我被他枪击后面对他时，一辆汽车从后面驶来，照亮了他的脸。他似乎透过我向远方凝视，有着一双极大、冰冷的蓝色眼睛。”辩护团队对瓦莱丽的回忆以及她据称对袭击者外貌特征（特别是头发和眼睛颜色）做出的不同描述进行了逐一反驳。由于她描述袭击者的所有书面记录在此后都已经丢失，她在法庭上的证词无法得到记录的支持。维也纳酒店房间里发现的弹壳——詹姆斯·汉拉蒂曾入住过该酒店——在法庭上进行了辩论

72
00:49:38.000 --> 00:50:16.000
in court. The prosecution argued that James had bought the gun just prior to the ASIC's murder. They claimed he practiced firing some shots, leaving the casings in the hotel room. His defence argued it was an unlikely scenario. However, the courtroom was about to hear more incriminating evidence against the James Hanratty. It came from his good friend, Charles Franz. As mentioned by author Paul Foote, three days before the trial began, Charles Franz attempted
在法庭上。控方辩称，詹姆斯在A6谋杀案发生前不久购买了这把枪。他们声称他练习过开枪，并将弹壳留在了酒店房间里。他的辩护律师则反驳说，这种情况不太可能发生。然而，法庭即将听到更多对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂不利的罪证。这些证据来自于他的好友查尔斯·弗朗茨。正如作家保罗·富特所提到的，在审判开始前三天，查尔斯·弗朗茨企图

73
00:50:16.000 --> 00:50:56.000
to take his own life and was admitted to hospital. His treating psychiatrist stated that his depression had been brought on by Charles introducing James Hanratty to his family. Charles allegedly told his doctor, the bastard, it could have been my dear wife and daughter who he killed. Charles appeared in court with two mental health nurses to give evidence against James and it was damning. He testified that a few weeks before the ASIC's murder, he and James had been at a dog track.
自杀并被送往医院。他的主治精神科医生表示，他的抑郁症是由于查尔斯将詹姆斯·汉拉蒂介绍给他的家人而引起的。查尔斯据称对他的医生说：“这个混蛋，被他杀的本可能是我的亲爱的妻子和女儿。”查尔斯在两名心理健康护士的陪同下出庭作证，指控詹姆斯的罪行，其证词极具毁灭性。他作证说，在A6谋杀案发生几周前，他和詹姆斯曾去过一个赛狗场。

74
00:50:56.000 --> 00:51:38.000
Afterwards, they caught the bus back to London. When they hopped on, it was empty. Charles went to take a seat at the front of the bus, but James motioned for him to sit toward the rear with him. Pointing to the back seat, Charles told the jury that James said words to the effect of, this is the only seat that lifts up and it's a good hiding place. Meanwhile, the investigation into James Hanratty's real alibi received a boost when the bed and breakfast he claimed to have stayed at was identified.
之后，他们乘公车返回伦敦。当他们跳上车时，车上空无一人。查尔斯走向前排座位坐下，但詹姆斯示意他跟自己一起坐到后排。查尔斯指着后排座位告诉陪审团，詹姆斯说出了类似这样的话：“这是唯一一个座位可以掀起来的地方，是个很好的藏身之处。”与此同时，随着被声称住过的民宿被确认，针对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂真实不在场证明的调查取得了进展。

75
00:51:38.000 --> 00:52:16.000
The landlady gave evidence that he had stayed there for two nights over the period of the ASIC's murder. However, she was a poor record keeper and did not have any documentation to support her claim. James described in detail the room he stayed in, including that it had a green bath. The prosecution claimed that the bed and breakfast was fully booked at the time. What they were unaware of and something the landlady reluctantly admitted at the trial was that there was an extra bed in one of the private bathrooms.
女房东出庭作证说，在A6谋杀案发生的期间，他在那里住了两个晚上。然而，她记账很潦草，没有任何文件来支持她的说法。詹姆斯详细描述了他住过的房间，包括里面有一个绿色的浴缸。控方声称，这家民宿当时已经全部客满。他们不知道、且女房东在审判中勉强承认的一点是，其中一个私人浴室里其实多放了一张床。

76
00:52:16.000 --> 00:52:59.000
It was given to guests when there were none others available. It had a green bath inside. The prosecution was without a clear motive in the case against James Hanratty. As Valerie Story had been raped, it was theorized the defendant was sexually motivated. However, there was never any inkling that James behaved violently towards women. Past girlfriends testified that James behaved like a perfect gentleman. One went so far as to label him as impeccable. Other women in his life described him similarly.
当没有其他房间可用时，这张床就会分配给客人。里面确实有一个绿色的浴缸。在对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂的指控中，控方没有确凿的动机。由于瓦莱丽·斯托里遭到强奸，外界推测被告是有性动机的。然而，从来没有任何迹象表明詹姆斯对女性表现出暴力倾向。过往的女友们作证说，詹姆斯表现得像个完美的绅士。其中一人甚至称他为“无可挑剔”。他生命中的其他女性对他的描述也大同小异。

77
00:52:59.000 --> 00:53:39.000
When James Hanratty took the stand, he answered questions calmly and without hesitation. At one stage, the prosecution compared his features to the identikit image, saying, Hanratty, do you always hold your right eyebrow higher than your left? To which he replied, I do not know, sir, because I cannot see it. His response prompted the courtroom to fill with laughter. James told the truth about his criminal past, saying, The man who committed this is a maniac and a savage.
当詹姆斯·汉拉蒂走上证人席时，他平静且毫不犹豫地回答了问题。某阶段，控方将他的外貌特征与模拟画像进行了对比，说道：“汉拉蒂，你是不是总是把右眉毛抬得比左眉毛高？”对此他回答说：“我不知道，长官，因为我看不到。”他的回答引得法庭哄堂大笑。詹姆斯坦白了自己过去的犯罪前科，说道：“犯下这桩罪行的人是个狂人和野兽。”

78
00:53:39.000 --> 00:54:24.000
I am not a man the court can approve of, but I am not a maniac of any kind. I can prove it with my past girlfriends. I am a decent, I cannot say honest, but I try to live a good and respectable life, except for my housebreakings. The trial against James Hanratty lasted 21 days. It was the longest against one defendant in British history. Over 80 witnesses were called to give evidence. On February 17, 1962, the jury retired for deliberations.
“我不是一个能让法庭认可的人，但我绝不是那种狂人。我可以拿我过去的女友们来证明这一点。我是个正派的人，我不能说自己有多诚实，但我努力过着美好而受人尊敬的生活，除了我的入室盗窃。”对詹姆斯·汉拉蒂的审判持续了21天。这是英国历史上针对单一被告历时最长的审判。法庭传唤了80多名证人出庭作证。1962年2月17日，陪审团退庭进行商议。

79
00:54:24.000 --> 00:55:10.000
Six hours later, they returned to ask for clarification of the phrase reasonable doubt. Another four hours passed before they reappeared. For the murder of Michael Grexton, James Hanratty was found guilty. The verdict sent shockwaves through the courtroom. The judge noticeably hesitated before delivering the sentence. James Hanratty was sentenced to death by hanging. In response, James' mother collapsed and fainted. James, visibly distressed, fell forward onto the dock.
六个小时后，他们返回法庭要求澄清“合理怀疑”这一短语的含义。又过了四个小时，他们才再次出现。因谋杀迈克尔·格雷克斯顿罪名成立，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂被判有罪。这一裁决在法庭上引起了轰动。法官在宣判前明显犹豫了一下。詹姆斯·汉拉蒂被判处绞刑。作为回应，詹姆斯的母亲当场虚脱昏倒。神情痛苦的詹姆斯向前扑倒在被告席上。

80
00:55:10.000 --> 00:56:05.000
He was asked if he had any final words to which he replied, I'm innocent my lord, I will appeal. That is all I have to say at this stage. Watching the chaos unfold in court was a man harboring a secret. He wanted to make a confession, but would have to wait a little longer. March 2, 1962 Dear mum, just a few lines in answer to your kind and welcome letter which I received the other day. I was very pleased to hear that you were all in good health and spirits at home.
当被问及是否有最后遗言时，他回答说：“我是清白的，法官大人，我会上诉。这就是我目前要说的一切。”正看着法庭上乱作一团的景象，有一个人心里藏着一个秘密。他很想坦白，但不得不再等上一小会儿。1962年3月2日亲爱的妈妈，简单几行字回复你前几天收到的亲切温暖的信。很高兴得知你们在家里的身体和精神状态都很好。

81
00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:49.000
Things are just the same here. I am over the shock now and I am patiently waiting for my appeal, which you already know is the 12th of March and I will be looking forward to seeing you then. I have had one or two letters from different people all expressing their faith in me and the outcome of the appeal. All I ask is when I go to court that I be given a fair trial and I feel this time the circumstances are different. I have great faith in my defence, he said to me, the guilty will be punished and the innocent set free. It is clear to thousands of people not only in here that the jury has made a terrible mistake.
这里的一切都和以前一样。我已经走出了震惊，正耐心地等待我的上诉，你们已经知道上诉日期是3月12日，我非常期待到时能见到你们。我收到了一封或两封不同的人写来的信，他们都对我和上诉结果表达了信心。我唯一的请求是，当我上诉时能得到公正的审判，我觉得这次情况有所不同。我对我的辩护律师很有信心，他对我说，有罪的人将受到惩罚，无罪的人将获得自由。不仅是在这里，成千上万的人都很清楚，陪审团犯了一个可怕的错误。

82
00:56:49.000 --> 00:57:40.000
I can't say at this stage that I am feeling happy, but I am keeping my spirits up and that is what really counts. Good night. God bless you all. James While James Hanradi awaited his appeal, his legal team had been busy trying to further verify his real alibi. They found an additional four witnesses who claimed to have seen a man resembling James in real at the time of the A6 murder. One recalled the strange colour of his hair, thinking it must have been a wig. However, James's legal team chose not to introduce these new witnesses at the appeal. Instead, they argued that the judge had misdirected the jury.
我现在还不能说自己感到快乐，但我一直在振作精神，这才是真正重要的事。晚安。上帝保佑你们所有人。詹姆斯在詹姆斯·汉拉蒂等待上诉期间，他的法律团队一直在忙于进一步核实他的真实不在场证明。他们找到了另外四名证人，他们声称在A6谋杀案发生时，在雷尔看到过一个长相酷似詹姆斯的男子。其中一人回忆起他头发的奇异颜色，以为那一定是一顶假发。然而，詹姆斯的法律团队选择不在上诉中引入这些新证人。相反，他们辩称法官对陪审团进行了错误的指导。

83
00:57:40.000 --> 00:58:18.000
The appeal was ultimately dismissed. As detailed by author Paul Foote, as the judge announced the dismissal, a woman in a wide-brimmed straw hat rose from her seat in the public gallery. She shouted, it's not true, he didn't do it, asked the conductor of the 36th bus. Everyone froze and turned to look at her. She took her seat again. The conductor of the A36 bus, where the murder weapon was located, did not recognise the woman or understand what she meant.
上诉最终被驳回。正如作家保罗·富特所详细描述的那样，当法官宣布驳回上诉时，旁听席上一位头戴宽边草帽的女士从座位上站了起来。她大喊道：“这不是真的，他没有干这事，去问36路公交车的售票员。”所有人都惊呆了，转过头去看着她。她重新坐回了座位。谋杀凶器所在的A36路公交车的售票员并不认识这位女士，也不明白她是什么意思。

84
00:58:18.000 --> 00:58:57.000
There was another female conductor who worked on that bus also. However, she was never questioned as to what the strange comment might mean. Watching from the public gallery was a man who had attended both the trial and the verdict. His friends had noticed a change in his behaviour that week. He became agitated with moments of hysterics. He had something that he wanted to get off his chest and ended up writing a letter to The Daily Express. It was published two days after James Hanratty's appeal was denied.
那辆公交车上还有另一位女售票员。然而，她从未被盘问过这个奇怪的评论可能意味着什么。旁听席上坐着一个旁听了审判和宣判全过程的男子。他的朋友们注意到他那一周的行为发生了变化。他变得焦躁不安，时而歇斯底里。他有些话如鲠在喉，最终写了一封信给《每日快报》。这封信在詹姆斯·汉拉蒂的上诉被驳回两天后发表。

85
00:58:57.000 --> 00:59:39.000
The letter read, I've studied the case very closely and I believe Hanratty was innocent. There has been so much confusion and doubt in this extraordinary case that I'm prepared to do all in my power to seek a reprieve. He signed it with his name, Peter Alfon. The next day, the landlady of a Doss house in Acton, London, called the police. In one of her rooms, she had discovered the body of a middle-aged man. A blue exercise book was by his body.
信中写道：“我非常仔细地研究了这个案子，我相信汉拉蒂是清白的。在这个非同寻常的案件中，有太多的混淆和疑点，我准备尽我所能寻求暂缓执行死刑。”他在信尾签下了自己的名字：彼得·阿尔丰。第二天，伦敦阿克顿一家收容所的女房东报警了。她在其中一个房间里发现了一名中年男子的尸体。尸体旁放着一本蓝色的练习本。

86
00:59:39.000 --> 01:00:18.000
It contained written letters to each of his family members. The letter to his wife, Charlotte, apologised for him bringing someone he thought to be a friend but was actually a monster into their house. It was the body of James Hanratty's long-time friend, Charles Frantz. Charlotte Frantz told The Sunday Times that the family had been pestered by calls in the lead-up to Charles' death. The caller had threatened, if Hanratty dies, you die. An inquest ruled Charles' death for suicide.
里面写着给他每个家庭成员的信。写给妻子夏洛蒂的信中，他为自己把一个他以为是朋友、实际上却是恶魔的人带进他们的家而道歉。死者是詹姆斯·汉拉蒂的老朋友查尔斯·弗朗茨。夏洛蒂·弗朗茨对《星期日泰晤士报》透露，在查尔斯死前的这段时间里，他们一家一直受到骚扰电话的纠缠。来电者威胁说：“如果汉拉蒂死，你也得死。”死因研讯裁定查尔斯为自杀。

87
01:00:18.000 --> 01:01:02.000
The judge refused to disclose the bulk contents of the letters Charles had penned to his loved ones. However, they were written, quote, in great bitterness and great feeling against Hanratty. Meanwhile, James Hanratty's execution date of April 4, 1962 loomed nearer. In a last-ditch effort to save him, James' legal time was over. James' legal team lodged a petition with the Home Office. They sought a pardon in light of the new witnesses that had come forward in real. On March 28, James' father handed in a petition to the Home Office.
法官拒绝透露查尔斯写给亲人们的信件的大部分内容。然而，信是用“对汉拉蒂极度怨恨和强烈情绪”的口吻写的。与此同时，詹姆斯·汉拉蒂定于1962年4月4日的行刑日期日益临近。为了拯救他的最后一搏，詹姆斯的法律团队耗尽了精力。詹姆斯的法律团队向内政部提交了一份请愿书。鉴于在雷尔出现的新证人，他们寻求获得特赦。3月28日，詹姆斯的父亲向内政部递交了一份请愿书。

